# =============================================================================
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash MXFP4 at 131072 -- gpu-experts, but at -ub 1024
# =============================================================================
# The gpu-experts profile with ONE variable changed, deliberately: -ub 1024
# instead of 8192, with --n-cpu-moe re-floored for it.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. Every one of the thirteen NaN aborts (RESULTS 32, TODO 16)
# happened at -ub >= 2048; none below. Counted in prefilled tokens rather than
# hours, and restricted to the no-rtr regime this profile uses:
#
#     -ub  512  rtr off   4 353 846 tokens   0 aborts
#     -ub 1024  rtr off     389 825 tokens   0 aborts
#     -ub 4096  rtr off   1 614 066 tokens   0 aborts
#     -ub 2048  rtr off     262 069 tokens   1 abort
#     -ub 8192  rtr off  10 428 857 tokens   4 aborts
#
# That is suggestive, not established -- see TODO 16 for why the arithmetic says
# 22.5 % coincidence. This profile exists to accumulate the volume that would
# settle it, on the SAME code path as the default, changing only -ub.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, measured 2026-08-20 (TODO 16.1):
#
#     configuration            pp 4k    pp 16k   pp 32k   tg 4k   tg 32k
#     gpu-experts -ub 8192 n19  1354      1901     1814   19.97    18.59
#     this profile  n17          372       376      369   21.42    20.16
#
# Prefill collapses to a fifth; generation gains 7-9 %. Both are the same effect:
# this strategy ships expert weights across PCIe every micro-batch, so a batch
# eight times smaller pays the transfer eight times as often for the same tokens.
# The giveaway is that prefill is flat across all three depths -- depth stops
# mattering once the link is the bottleneck. A 32k prompt costs 89 s of prefill
# here against 18 s on the default.
#
# If prefill matters to you, deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram is faster at this same
# -ub and the same n17 (478 / 486 pp, 21.70 / 20.32 tg) because it computes
# experts on the CPU via -rtr and never touches the link. But that is a different
# code path, so it does NOT test what this profile tests.
#
# AN EXPERIMENT, NOT A RECOMMENDATION. It is in the repository so the numbers in
# TODO 16 can be re-run rather than reconstructed, not because anyone should
# serve from it. TODO 16.2 suggests -ub 4096 as the better bargain if the point
# is running rather than testing.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-MXFP4-00001-of-*.gguf}"
: "${IK_ALIAS:=deepseek-v4-flash-gpu-experts-128k-ub1024}"
: "${IK_CTX:=131072}"

# The flag that defines the gpu-experts family: experts computed on the GPU.
: "${IK_RTR:=0}"

: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
# 17, not 19. -ub 1024 needs ~880 MiB of compute buffer against 7040 at 8192, and
# the ~6 GiB freed buys two expert layers back onto the GPU. MEASURED, not
# inferred: n17 loads and serves 32k; n16 and n15 both fail with out of memory.
# Worth 9 % of prefill and 8 % of generation over leaving it at 19.
: "${IK_NCMOE:=17}"

: "${IK_UBATCH:=1024}"
# -b stays 8192. It is only an upper bound on -ub, so it does not clamp anything
# here, and leaving it matched to the default profile keeps the diff to one line.
: "${IK_BATCH:=8192}"

: "${IK_CTK:=f16}"
: "${IK_CTV:=f16}"

# -nkvo is still required: with KV on the GPU the attention scratch follows it and
# nothing fits. See the default profile for the measurement.
: "${IK_EXTRA_ARGS:=-mla 3 -fidx --reasoning-format deepseek -nkvo}"
